body: "## Problem\n\nThe memory privacy scanner judges credential values that sit under a credential key inside **JSON** objects, including numeric values. When the text is not valid JSON, for example a single-quoted object such as `{'password': {'value': 123456789}}`, the fallback checks only the immediate value after the key (`{`) and never inspects its descendants. So `remember` admits a numeric credential that the JSON path would refuse, and the pinned SDK's container walk also judges only quoted strings.\n\nThis happens on `main` and on pm-memory#51's head. Probe on 2026-11-13: `privacy(\"{'password': {'value': 123456789}}\")` is admitted by both. It was raised in review as PRRT_kwDOVPw2oc626hoD.\n\n## Context\n\npm-memory#51 adds several bounded fallbacks for non-JSON assignment grammars, and each review round has found another spelling the fallbacks miss. This item covers the **class**: credential descendants in non-JSON containers.\n\n## Expected\n\n- One bounded, linear walker for single-quoted and otherwise non-JSON object and array literals. Every scalar descendant under a credential key, numbers included, goes through the same `credentialLiteral` rules as the JSON path.\n- One shared corpus that both the JSON path and the fallback path must refuse, so the two paths cannot drift: nested objects, arrays, numbers, quoted and unquoted keys, escaped quotes.\n- Must-allow controls: placeholders and references (`<secret-manager-reference>`, `${X}`), and ordinary prose that mentions the word \"password\".\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- `{'password': {'value': 123456789}}` and its array and nested variants are refused on both paths, and the shared corpus passes on both.\n- The walker stays linear on 100,000-character inputs, so CodeQL reports no polynomial regex.\n\n\n---\n\n### GitHub comments (2)\n\n> **@unbraind** (2026-11-13T04:51:50Z)\n>\n> Widening the scope to the whole class: **the non-JSON assignment fallback grammar**. pm-memory#51 fixed these spellings one review round at a time:\n> - a literal after a quoted reference suffix;\n> - a bracket-wrapped literal after a quoted, closed-quote or unquoted reference;\n> - a reference truncated by the scalar matcher;\n> - a quadratic rescan of repeated reference assignments;\n> - a literal split across short adjacent segments.\n> \n> Each round found the next one, which means the fallback needs one grammar and one shared corpus rather than more special cases. Scope for this issue:\n> 1. A single bounded tokenizer for assignment values (references, scalars, quoted segments, `<…>` wrappers, annotations), reused by the JSON-adjacent and the fallback paths.\n> 2. One corpus that both paths must refuse, including numeric descendants in single-quoted containers (the original report here), plus the must-allow controls (complete references, placeholders, prose).\n> 3. A linear-time bound on 500 KB adversarial inputs.\n> \n> From now on, a newly reported spelling in this class that `main` also admits is tracked here instead of in #51.\n\n> **@unbraind** (2026-11-13T06:54:43Z)\n>\n> Another spelling of the class, from pm-memory#51's review at 39c19f0 (PRRT_kwDOVPw2oc62_D0R). In `password=\"${X}ab\"\"cdefgh\"`, the literal that continues *inside* the initial reference quote (`ab`) is not carried into the adjacent run, so `abcdefgh` is never judged as one value. A probe on 2026-11-13 shows that `main` and #51's head both admit it. Add it to the shared corpus; the unified tokenizer handles it once in-quote continuations and adjacent segments are one token stream."
